Detailed Solution:
Hierarchical inheritance involves multiple subclasses inheriting from a single superclass.
Java Code:
class Employee {
    String name;
    int id;
    Employee(String name, int id) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = id;
    }
    void calculateSalary() {
        System.out.println("Base employee salary calculation");
    }
}
class PermanentEmployee extends Employee {
    double basicPay;
    PermanentEmployee(String name, int id, double basicPay) {
        super(name, id);
        this.basicPay = basicPay;
    }
    void calculateSalary() {
        double gross = basicPay + 0.5 * basicPay;
        System.out.println(name + " Permanent Salary: " + gross);
    }
}
class ContractEmployee extends Employee {
    int hours;
    double rate;
    ContractEmployee(String name, int id, int hours, double rate) {
        super(name, id);
        this.hours = hours;
        this.rate = rate;
    }
    void calculateSalary() {
        System.out.println(name + " Contract Salary: " + (hours * rate));
    }
}
public class HierarchicalTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e1 = new PermanentEmployee("Rohan", 1, 50000);
        Employee e2 = new ContractEmployee("Priya", 2, 40, 500);
        e1.calculateSalary();
        e2.calculateSalary();
    }
}

Detailed Solution:
The super keyword in Java is a reference variable used to refer to the immediate parent class object.
1. Accessing parent variables: When subclass hides parent variable with the same name, super.variable accesses the parent's field.
2. Invoking parent methods: super.methodName() calls the overridden version in the parent class.
3. Invoking parent constructors: super(...) invokes the superclass constructor during object instantiation (must be first statement in constructor).
Code Snippet Example:
class Parent {
    int x = 10;
    Parent() { System.out.println("Parent constructor"); }
    void show() { System.out.println("Parent show"); }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    int x = 20;
    Child() {
        super(); // 3. Constructor invocation
        System.out.println("Parent x: " + super.x); // 1. Variable access
    }
    void show() {
        super.show(); // 2. Method invocation
        System.out.println("Child show");
    }
}

